A||||8||88formula||I need your thoughts about  ACE||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||01-14-2001||11:08 PM||||What do you guys think about American Custom Engineering (ACE)? I have be hearing some pretty negative things about this company and the services they provide but I just cannot believe that this true. Can you people who have had services done by these guys give me your thoughts?<P>Thanks<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||bHooper||01-15-2001||06:56 AM||||theres a thread from a few weeks ago... please do a search for this topic<P>hoop<p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000002||stimpy||01-15-2001||03:15 PM||||Why is it that you "can't believe (negtive feedback) to be true"?  I have seen firsthand the results of an ACE conversion and was far from impressed.  Serious lack of engineering. There is more to doing a swap then making an engine fit.  Look on their website for the yellow 86 GT for sale. Look at the engine pic and notice the white marks on the decklid over the alternator. Notice how there is no torque strut. Those white markes are gouges in the decklid from the alternator pulley from the engine lifting into the deck on acceleration. Think about the stress put on the tranny, lower engine mounts, exhaust. All of these parts had to be repaired on the 3.8 conversion that I saw, not to mention replaceent and relocation of the coil pack due to poor placement during the original swap.<BR>If you are thinking about sending your car to Colorado, I highly recommend that you send it the extra coupla hundered miles to Chris West  <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  and have it done right! <p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||01-15-2001||03:16 PM||||Doh! Double post.<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||01-15-2001||04:05 PM||||My ACE conversion has served me well for the last 40,000 miles. (I did add a torque strut)||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||01-15-2001||05:14 PM||||Doing a search is too much work. <P>Stimpy I cannot believe that if Ace really did poor work that they would be in business for very long. On there web-site they emphasize quality and doing the job write. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||01-15-2001||11:00 PM||||Talk is cheap, and bandwidth is cheaper.  The car that I referred to with the "issues" belongs to my dad, so I got some up close and personal time with it. I would not go as far as saying they are con artists or a ripoff, but when ACE says the car is "done", it is actually far from it, and they really don't display an attitude of caring about customer support after the sale. Don't let me stop you, but my personal endorsement remains West Coast Fieros.<P>PS, now that my dad has fabricated a torque strut, relocated the coil pack, re-welded the exhaust, repaired his cracked motor mount,and had his allegedly zero-mile(per ACE)<BR>tranny rebuilt, he is actually quite happy with his ACE conversion as well. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric_PA||01-16-2001||06:45 AM||||I posted a topic about this a few weeks ago with a lot of negative feelings about ACE. I am kind of stuck, I have a HO QUAD 4 laying around now for like 2 years and still didn't put it in. It looks like I will probably be relying on them for the kit. In fact I talked to Richard Cutter last night on the phone with many questions. He was not short with me at all and answered all my questions I had and explained all involved with the swap. I'm thinking about giving them a try even though some have sour feelings aboiut them. It's just scarry to pay that money to them before you see results. Before I jump into going to them, there is one more engine kit source I have to talk to today. I am contacting IRM to talk to Jim Sutter. He has some parts laying around yet, but does not offer a kit anymore. If he has the Engine mount and documentation I will be kicking the ACE option to the curb. <BR>Here is the link to the ACE topic I posted <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005063.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005063.html</A> <P>Eric<BR>Black 86.5 Fastback Ratmatic ---&gt; HO Quad 5 speed<BR>Red 84 4 speed <p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 01-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dan86gt3.8||01-16-2001||12:36 PM||||The only gripe I have about ace is they took forever to get my stuff to me.  But once it was there everything went fine.  But I now have heard that he has upped the price of the kit, and I don't think its worth that much anymore.  You should buy the motor mounts from chris west and get somebody to do the wiring.  Everything else is relitively simple to figure out ( a radiator hose here and there, a little fuel line bending, couple vacum lines to be hooked up and youre done).  I mean thats just my opinion thoguh, if you want to spend the money almost everything you need is given to you and you don't have to wory about any wiring.  Do it yourself because its not that hard, I find that most people that take it out and let him do everything, a) pay alott B) are always pissed about quality of engines/trannys bought by him or his work.  <P>ps- for whoever made that comment about his shop being part of his house WTF?  To run a buisness does it require that you drive 15 minutes to a place thats in a buisness district.  Do you also need a lift and stuff to be a real buisness?  Cause if so discredit John lingenfelter too, because he works of jackstands.  Also how could a shop attached to a house build and work on a winston west full out race car?  I guess its not really a stockcar if you build it in a nieghborhood.  I know this man, because I visited it.  That remark was about the most retarted, assinine thing I have heard in awhile.<P>-Dan<BR>-owner of a 3.8 fiero, built in a resedintial area, if you could believe that...<BR> <P>------------------<BR>86 gt fiero<BR>3.8/4speed auto<BR>ace conversion kit<BR>1/4- 15.3@90mph||||reg||1
